WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
well i'm joined now by omid scobee  co-author of  finding freedom great to see you thanks  for being with us on sky news it's an  interesting  title isn't it that you've chosen for  this book finding freedom  why is it called that well i think when  you look at what freedom really means  freedom to express yourself or speak up  on anything that you want that's  something that the sussexes weren't  always able to do within the institution  of the monarchy and  certainly where we look at where they're  at today talking about systemic racism  and gender equity in a way that we  hadn't seen them before it looks like  they have found some form of it  and you began the book uh a couple of  years ago but of course  uh the story changed dramatically didn't  it from what from when you  you began to write chapter one  yeah this really started out as the  chronicle of their first year of  marriage and their courtship and of  course those first days with archie  leading up to that tour in southern  africa  and of course as time went by we  realized a bigger story was building  behind the scenes and  increasingly sort of unhappy couple were  in front of us  and we found out why that was you know  it was within the institution of the  monarchy that they felt extremely  frustrated and  under-supported and under-defended from  some of the more vicious  tabloid attacks too some of the  anecdotes and stories in the book  are incredibly personal aren't they um i  know they've denied it but did you speak  with harry and megan  directly no we didn't speak with harry  megan directly  they didn't cooperate with the book and  there's certainly no secret off the  record conversations that have taken  place either  but i think the unique situation that we  had here were many of the people in  harry and megan's lives felt  increasingly frustrated  with some of the coverage that they saw  on the people that they knew that looked  so  different on the pages of some sections  of the british tabloids and  i think there was this real desire to  speak out and defense of the couple  especially when the palace wasn't doing  so themselves  uh because you know as well as i do when  journalists talk about close friends  uh uh uh and then make statements what  they actually mean is that the people  themselves  no and i understand that there's this  sort of desire to  make this the next andrew morton book  and unfortunately there is no  uh secret delivery of audio cassette  recordings from harry and megan or  private diaries or anything like that  we really have spoken to the people  close to them who are their friends who  are the people that have worked for them  past and present  those include many palace aides as well  themselves and  to also get balance we spoke with those  close to the cambridges the prince of  wales the queen herself  and so that really gave a balanced  overview of exactly what went wrong  yeah i suppose that that has been one of  the criticisms of the book  that that it's quite one-sided coming  from from from  very much from the couple's point of  view and not all the reviews have been  kind i'm sure you'll be aware of this  one today the only winners from this  oily guff of the authors cashing in  that was uh richard k in the daily mail  what did you make of that  i mean it's interesting words from a  journalist who's cashed in on their  relationship with the princess  princess diana but um i mean listen  everyone's entitled to an opinion  on this book and i think one thing that  we had really heard about harry and  megan time and time again  was the other side of the story from  within the palace we knew what other  royal family members thought of them  we knew what aids were briefing certain  sections of the press  anonymously and i think that this was a  chance just to add to that landscape  a different perspective so at least when  we look at it all we now have a much  more balanced overview of the situation  yeah and for a couple that have clearly  craved their  privacy they've wanted to keep their  private life their personal life private  i mean this book is anything but  yeah i think that there are obviously a  lot of details in here about the couple  and i really wanted to re-humanize them  in a way because it felt like for a long  time that we had  sort of lost a sense of who they were  they'd become caricatures  in certain sections of the press and i  think that this does  bring a human quality to the couple  again you know we have to remember  they are a couple that understand the  intense interest  in their lives both publicly and  privately you know only today we saw  them  confirm to a number of news  organizations that they had bought a  home in santa barbara so  they are willing to share certain  details about their lives i think  they're also not afraid to call on the  law when they see those lines crossed  yeah when it comes to  you can see you can sort of see how  people in the uk will be  raising their eyebrows if you like that  for a couple who have  said for so long they want privacy they  want to be out of the limelight  for them to then move to los angeles  which is the center of celebrity the  center of the paparazzi  is slightly strange well santa barbara  is actually a hundred miles from los  angeles so they're not in los angeles or  parts of hollywood  they're in california showbiz place  isn't it  california is one of the largest states  in america so it's hard to really call  let's show this place it is where  megan's mother lives and i think for  them as a family  picking a place where really they were  able to lay their roots and have that  support system around them was very  important  and of course when we look at america it  has a huge charitable landscape and of  course  their archwell organization which  they'll be launching  later in the year is one of the big  driving factors in the work that they're  doing  yeah um the book says megan says i gave  up my entire life for this family  it was her choice to join the royal  family though  wasn't it um she did go in with her eyes  open  absolutely and i think that there's also  a difference between knowing  what you have up ahead of you and  actually experiencing it for yourself  and  you know listen if there's one thing  that we've seen about megan she really  does give it her all she's not a quitter  and i think that  it has been this big common  misconception that  she was the one in the driver's seat for  this decision to step away and i think  in reality when we really look at the  information and  everything that we already know about  harry this was a world in which i think  he wanted to step away from anyway and  megan was someone that simply gave him  the support and confidence within that  but  i would also argue that if he had said  let's stick it out a few more years i  would imagine she would have done that  too  and just very briefly um omid how will  the story end you think  will they return to the uk one day uh  will harry want to  come back into the royal family at some  stage  i mean the queen's made it very clear to  harry and megan that the door is always  open for them to come back they're  obviously  currently in a 12-month probation period  that will be discussed  next easter to see whether it is that  they would want to come back i think  when we look at them now though they  seem very forward focused they're laying  their roots in the us  and i think the uk will play a big role  in their lives when it comes to their  patronages and of course  the family they have over here but not  as working members of it  okay i mean scobee co-author of finding  freedom we must leave it there thank you  very much

